A||||26||fyerstarter||Howto: Custom Cold Air Induction for less than $100||||1||
Z||000000||fyerstarter||03-23-2001||09:07 PM||||Sup everyone. I just discovered this board and holy sh*t this place is active! I think I may have found my new favorite hangout.<P>Anyway I'll introduce myself, I'm 20 years old, I'm a video game artist by trade, and I own a modified 87 Gt.<P>So far I've:<P>-Lowered<BR>-Headers<BR>-3 angle valve job<BR>-Intake<BR>-Flowmaster Exhaust<BR>-Custom Flushmount Headlights (crappy self done job, Im looking for the real ones)<BR>-Custom Tail Lights<BR>-Short Shifter<BR>-Shaved Emblems and holes where the wing used to be<P>I'll post a pic when I get some recent ones. She still looks pretty ugly cuz I'm rollin primer but soon to be painted.<P>Anyway! Now that I have lulled you all into a slumber back on topic. Lately I've seen some intakes being offered for the Fiero at  upwards of $300. I'm here to tell you how to do it for less than $100<P>Here's what you need.<P>-Honda Civic Cold Air Intake (they just started selling these at Autozone for $60 for the tube and $20 for the filter)<BR>-3 long piece of 3" wide thinwall pipe (Home Depot)<BR>-3" wide 90 degree rubber elbow (Home Depot)<BR>-4 Hose Clamps (one comes with the intake, one with the filter, and there is one on the stock intake so you only need to buy 1 more  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Tip:  Look around the plumbing and ductwork section and you can find most everything you need  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Okay after you have that stuff let's get started:<P>1. Remove your stock airbox, the little rain filter deal, the stock intake snorkel, everything from the throttle body down! Deposit it in the nearest waste receptacle.<P>2. Attach the Civic intake tube to the throttle body(if you cant figure out how to do this maybe you should pay $350 :P)  aim the end of it so that it is right in front of the hole that goes out to the air intake vent on the side of the Fiero.<P>3. Jack up the car and take off the back tire. Remove the fenderwell skirt. (You probably already had to do this to get out the stock air intake crap)<P>4. Attach the 90 degree elbow to the end of the Civic Intake tube, it should go through the hole in side and come out right behind the intake vent.<P>5. Attach the cone filter to the 3" piece of piping you bought and stick the other end in the rubber elbow. Adjust it all so that the center of the filter is exactly positioned behind the intake vent (If you got one of those cheezy cone filters with a cap on it go back and get the one that sucks through the center too). <P>6. Now Hose clamp it all down.<P>You will need to fab a bracket to keep it from bouncing all over the place but thats not too hard. <P>ALSO!  You will need to cut the left decklid support in order for the decklid to close. So if you want your decklid to stay open you'll need to fab up a little prop for it (you know like on all the jap car hoods these days).<P>I was into rice rockets (Japanese cars) for quite a few years and adding an intake was not that big of a mod. Thats because most of the time the filter is located inside the stuffy ol hot engine compartment. On this setup it is sucking nothing but clean cold air from outside and you even get a litte ram air action going on at the higher speeds  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I was pleasantly surprised by the improvement.<P><BR>Okay now for my ulterior motives for offering this cornicopia of my knowledge :P I have a request. Does anyone know where I can find some flushmount headlights for the Fiero exactly like the ones at Garys Custom Exotics,  <A HREF="http://www.gscustom.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gscustom.com/</A>    Now don't get me wrong the ones at Gary's are cool and those are the ones I'm planning on buying right now. But he has no email address on his site or anything. I just have a sneaking suspicion that whenever I get around to calling him up they will be out of business or no longer selling them and I'll be screwed. Anyway if anyone knows of some other places to get similar headlights I'd certainly appreciate the info.<P>Thanks alot. You'll prob be seeing more of me around here. <P>Fyerstarter out.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||GoCartGuy||03-23-2001||09:17 PM||||Great post fyerstarter, but I have one addition to add.  For those of you trying this my recommendation is to get a heat sheilded cone filter.  Toucan Industries makes one, paid $30 for it. I had a similar set up on my V-6 and when I went to do the last filter change noticed that I had melted part of the filter material on one side, not a big spot, but enough to annoy me.  So I slipped over to one of my local auto shops and asked for assistance.  Anyways, the said that these work great in the Fieros with a remote battery mount, mine's up front.  Big bonus, it comes in cool color, got one that was close to my color...yellow.  I'll keep you all posted if I have any problems.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tryxalon||03-24-2001||05:50 PM||||I'm not sure that your modification is actually getting any MORE air into the engine. <P>The water separator forces the incoming air through a specific path up and over a fence, the air travels some 27" between the outer grill of the intake and the connection to the "snorkle" that leads to the air cleaner. The "screwdriver sized hole" that everyone THINKS the air is going through is a condensation weep hole.  <P>I seem to remember some tests made back in the early (83-85) days by someone (Holley?) that proved the stock intake system was actually superior to a "straight pipe" because of air flow, pressure scavenging and velocity.  <P>Any experts remeber?? ||||reg||5
Z||000003||Sleeper||03-24-2001||06:07 PM||||in my opinion, or should i say my friends experience the heat shielded air filters are junk especially the ones with the shield that goes all the way around the filter. they don't have a good maximum air flow and thats what you want right? if you do the cold intake mod you should get a OPEN END air filter, K&N has them, A'PEXi has a really nice one, HKS and Greddy have superflow foam mesh ones. you just gotta spend the $$$.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fyerstarter||03-24-2001||06:32 PM||||Tryxalon:<P>If you look at all the stock intake setup I cannot see any advantage to having all of those restrictions, bends, kinks, filters, etc. over a straight pipe with an open end filter located outside of the engine compartment.<P>I remember reading the same test I think, but the comparison was with the stock intake setup and nothing at all. Of course it's not going to do too well if its sucking nice hot engine compartment air in right through the throttle body. Same goes with intake setups where the filter is located inside the engine compartment. <P>Anyway I was skeptacle that there would be any performance gain too when I started other than the cool sucking noise and better exhaust note but like I said, I was pleasantly surprised. I haven't been able to dyno test it nor do I have the time slips to prove it but I can certainly feel the difference, there is less hesitation, the rpms gain quicker, it feels like I pulled the sock out of her throat the she's been gagging on :P<P>But anyway for those of you who do decide to do this mod just don't spend $350 at the fiero store when you can do a better setup yourself in under an hour for $100  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||fyerstarter||03-24-2001||06:34 PM||||I see no reason for a heat shielded filter either. In my setup the filter is located outside the engine compartment inside the rear quarter panel directly behind the intake vent. Where is the heat coming from that you need to protect it from?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Joe4x4||03-24-2001||06:47 PM||||Do you have pic?<P>------------------<BR>Need For Speed........<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/car1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||03-24-2001||06:55 PM||||I did the one in my car and many others incl fiero lisa's cheaper than that.<P>Keep the stock rubber elbow , <P>Get a chrome "Boom tube" exhaust tip , and a K&N conical filter...<P><BR>came to $68 total I believe. Search the forum for the exact instructions<P>back to bed<BR>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||03-24-2001||06:59 PM||||Here we go ... Now I'm goin back to bed.... cheap cost , not complicated , looks good. I did my first one about 5 years ago , installed around 15 of them to date and sold a few.<P>From a previous post....<P><BR>May as well explain it all.....<P>1st ..is it worth it??? Good question , most people have found they lost as bit of TQ , I have only ever seen 2 actual people put their cars on the dyno. My friend Mark waters was the second car I put a cold air kit onto. He has a 2.8 , ADS chip , MSD box , really clean car , well thought out. He gained 6Hp @ the wheels , but lost 7ft lbs tq. The result basically was a motor that was a bit more happy to rev, fiero's seem to have a Hump in the powerband.<P>Either way it's a cheap mod , The car makes really nifty induction sounds (haha) and for my purposes it helped , I autocross a lot engine is always between 4500 and 6000rpm.<P>Basically what you need to do:<P>Jack up the car , remove the drivers side rear wheel. Remove the inner fender liner.<P>there is a black air inlet box , it connects to the outside air inlet vent via a little rubber gasket. Keep everything you never know when someone may need it.<P>The air box is hard to get out , there is a nut up high that will take some work. Once you get the air box out , the hard part is over. Sometimes you have to break to box to get it out. My car had 20,000 original miles on it when I did the air induction and the bolt was rusted...It was very difficult to get it out. Also you need to remove all related ain intake items.. Air canister , everything! , when your done you will just have a hole in the rear inner fender where your canister sat.<P><BR>Now what you need to get is a chrome "Boom Tube" exhaust tip , these can be found at autozone , for canadians Crappy tire has these. The boom tube you want is 18" long with a 3" ID (inside diameter) , Now this will have a reduced end on it , and a slash cut end. Cut these off so it is a chrome cylinder roughly 11-13" long depending on the K&N filter you get.<P>The filter should be a K&N conical filter , this is the beauty of the system , the filter as we all know from their ads is washable and lasts forever. <P>Tip: Washing a K&N ..don't waste money on K&N's cleaner system , get the filter oil and wash it in the dishwasher. I thought this was a goofy idea until I saw a Ferrari cup car mechanic do it..case closed.<P><BR>Ok Now you have all original air intake parts out. You need the original sensor that is in the air canister. Drill a hole in your chrome pipe and have a nut , or exhaust bing welded in so that the sensor can be threaded in easily. Don't have access to a welder...just thread it into the pipe hole..<P><BR>Now when you hook this all up your going to use the original Throttle body rubber elbow ..this is the one that comes off the TB and goes into the canister. The nice part is that it keeps everything lookin clean , not a hack job. The Elbow should be off of the TB.<P>You need to attach the filter to the fender and feed the chrome tube up through the hole in the inner fender.. This is best done as a 2 person job , someone needs to hold the chrome tube up top so that it doesn't fall back down into the fender. So with that done attach the chrome tube to the rubber elbow.<P>MAKE SURE TO TEST FIT THE ELBOW AND RUBBER TUBE!<P>It may take a little finessing... Everytime I do this it seems to vary based on milage of the car, ie rubber shrinks with heat.<P>This is easy use a dremel drill to take a bit of the rubber on the inside of the elbow out.. a shot of liquid wrench.. it should slide on snugly ..be patient!.<P>Now you have the filter , connected to the chrome tube , connected to the rubber elbow. Now it's time to connect it to the TB. , If you take your time it will connect up to the TB with little or no trouble , the metal line will connect back up to the Rubber TB elbow.<P>Re-connect sensor and your done almost.<P>If you did the above right the filter will be viewable through the side vent. The problem is that you need to build a splash shield for the filter or else the filter gets extremely dirty. I made mine out of a rubber plumbing mat and sealed it with caulking... air tight<P>IF it won't reconnect to the TB easily... shorten the Chrome tube a bit ..it will fit , again be patient. The result is a clean looking install that most pople won't notice. I find the conversion useful because on the track I spend 90% of my time between 4500 and 6300rpm<P>I think thats about it. If anyone has any questions let me know<P><BR>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||03-24-2001||07:04 PM||||all those restrictions in the air box have an effect on the Torque of the car, as well they muffle the air intake noise and keep the car from sounding like a hoover at idle ..like I said above , most guys loose 6 to 9ft lbs of TQ . Heat shielded Air cleaners are junk. if you want to do it right , get the filter right out of the engine compartment and into the fender where the Air Box used to sit. The noise you hear isn't a change in the exhaust , its a change in the air intake path ..just induction noise.<P><BR>go to <BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fierolisa</A> <P>I did the conversion on her car about a month ago , there are some pics of the conversion on the page under "My fiero's" then go to the section called "My 86 GT" you can see a pic of me installing the system.<P><BR>JM<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||DXR_DAD||03-24-2001||07:14 PM||||This is a little different  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I don't want to jack up car, remove wheel well, or do any extra work to replace a filter???? <P>I posted part of this in another post a while back, I'm still working on it but here are a few pics:<P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=12120996&p=43932246&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=12120996&p=43927731&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=12120996&p=43932247&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=12120996&p=43928994&Sequence=0&res=high"> ||||reg||10
Z||000011||Slammed Fiero||03-24-2001||07:20 PM||||Filter looks like it's right in the nice hot engine bay <P>Hmmm<P>JM||||reg||1
