Z||000024||c m west||04-07-2001||04:04 PM||||the Air Inlet Tempurature sensor needs to be in the incoming airflow to properly function, this is why every Cold Air Intake Tube we sell here at the shop has a welded in fitting for the sensor.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000025||Slammed Fiero||04-07-2001||05:02 PM||||I have the sensor with the fitting welded in , my question pertains to the use of a resistor or potentiometer to fool the ECm into thining it's getting a lot cooler air intake charge<P>JM ||||reg||10
Z||000026||fyerstarter||04-07-2001||05:18 PM||||The civic intake that I used had a capped hole in it exactly the size of the sensor. I simply removed the sensor from the old airbox and secured it into the hole with a nut on the inside and some rubber o-rings to keep it air-tight. Also I got a little breather filter to cap off the valve cover breather hose.<P>I took some pics I just gotta get em developed they'll be up soon.<P>Eric: Hey my name is Erik too. Hehe. Actually I have been contemplating checkin you guys out since your not too far from here. I wanna see that red beast in person  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> And possibly having you guys do some work on my GT. I've got the dreaded rod knock so I'm having visions of a 3.4 conversion or possible at high performace rebuild to the 2.8  <P>But sadly it's crunch time here at work so I'll be workin the next six weekends  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Anyway I'll keep in touch and I'm definitely down for any fiero festivities here on the west coast after this game ships and I finally get my life back  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
